{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's central mechanism attributes the reliability gain to 'cognitive friction' that forces serial verification and eliminates expert blind spots. But the problem specifically demands that the argument identify what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. SRC_001's mechanism gives no diagnostic criterion: any observed correctness of the panel is equally consistent with (a) genuine friction-driven error correction and (b) rote checklist adherence that coincidentally matches the verifiable outcome. The counterconditions (vague checklist causing semantic drift; holistic-synthesis tasks) describe failure modes, not the discriminating evidence the problem asks for. A single evaluation showing the panel outperforming the expert could not, per SRC_001's own mechanism, separate whether the gain came from friction-recovered visibility versus the checklist simply encoding the answer path—which is precisely the confound the problem requires the argument to address.","counterexample":[{"confound":"same result would occur if checklist mechanically encoded the correct procedure without any genuine error-recovery, so the evidence cannot distinguish the two explanations","expert_correct":false,"friction_present":true,"outcome":"panel produces correct judgment via strict step-by-step checklist adherence","panel_correct":true}],"target":"abe7829a82928d8a6b1507f6258e5d03336d27cd2fee40c85149191860723d56"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 offers a genuine test: real reliability gain occurs only when semantic drift lets the panel catch errors the checklist author was blind to. But this test is self-defeating as a discriminating criterion within a single evaluation. The problem asks for evidence that separates genuine gain from mere compliance that tracks the right answer. Under SRC_002's own framing, when the panel's divergent interpretation h